<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  A Signal K server on a boat is almost always plain HTTP on a private network
  (the units in this project talk to http://192.168.0.100:3000). Android blocks
  cleartext by default since API 28, so it has to be permitted explicitly.

  This file used to scope that permission to the RFC1918 ranges with eighteen
  entries of the form <domain includeSubdomains="true">192.168.</domain>, on the
  reasoning that "domain rules match by suffix, so the leading octets cover the
  whole range". That reasoning is inverted, and none of those entries ever
  matched anything. Android matches a domain by SUFFIX: the hostname must equal
  it, or end with "." + it. That is correct for DNS names, where example.com
  should cover api.example.com. An IP address's network part is a PREFIX, so
  192.168.0.100 does not end with ".192.168" and never matched. Every attempt to
  reach a boat server failed with "CLEARTEXT communication to 192.168.0.100 not
  permitted by network security policy".

  It cannot be fixed here. Android's network security config has no CIDR or
  range syntax; the only things that can be written are exact hosts and DNS
  suffixes, and the server's address is not known at build time.

  So the restriction moved to where it can be expressed AND tested:
  isPrivateHost() in the pure :core module, checked via
  ServerAddress.isCleartextSafe before any address is used. The app refuses to
  talk cleartext to anything that is not on a private network, which is the
  property this file was trying and failing to enforce. Following AGENTS.md,
  that is where it belonged in the first place: it is a decision, and decisions
  live in the host-tested core rather than in platform configuration.

  Note for anyone editing this file: a double dash is illegal inside an XML
  comment, so the project's usual em-dash style cannot be used here.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
